# E2E testing — when to use it

## Use e2e for
- Critical user journeys (signup/login, checkout, create-then-list)
- Cross-layer wiring (UI → API → DB → email/webhook)
- Regressions that only show up in a real browser or real network
- Thin release smoke (few paths, high confidence the build is alive)

## Prefer unit or integration for
- Pure logic and pure functions
- Single-service contracts and schema checks
- Assertions that need heavy or flaky infrastructure

## Rule of thumb
Keep a **small** set of high-value e2e tests. Push everything else down the pyramid (unit → integration → e2e).

When adding a test, ask: does this need a full stack to catch the bug? If not, do not make it e2e.
